{0 Making a game on CamlCast}

This guide builds a small game, {e The Undercroft}, from an empty directory,
one feature per step. The finished game has a dark vault, a brazier, torches
operated by gaze, a gate raised by a winch, and an exit under an evening sky.

A game on this engine {e describes} its world. Every frame it states, from
nothing, what the world should be right now, and the runtime computes what
changed. This is React's model applied to a raycaster. The engine therefore
already provides the two things a game usually has to invent for itself: state
storage and a way to get state onto the screen.

{b Every step of this guide is a complete program.} The page quotes only what
each step adds. The whole game as each step leaves it is
[examples/stepNN_<slug>.ml], compiled with the rest of this repository so it
cannot drift from the engine. From a checkout,

{@sh[
dune build
dune exec examples/step01_room.exe
]}

runs any step. Run [dune build] first; it puts [assets/] where the later steps
can read it.

{1 Four things to know before the first line}

{2 There is no level file format}

A level is OCaml code. There is no [.map] to parse and nothing to load at
start-up: a room is a value, a world is a value, and a level that varies with
the time of day is a function.

There {e is} an editor, and it does not change that sentence. It draws the room
you are standing in, lets you take hold of a corner, and writes the coordinate
back into the line of your own source that put it there — one number changed
and the file otherwise byte for byte. What it saves is what you would have
typed, which is why there is still nothing to parse and nothing to load.

It is turned on properly at step 23. Before then it is mentioned where it would
have helped, because the point of writing these twenty-odd programs by hand is
to learn what the tool is writing.

{2 The engine holds no content}

The engine ships no colour, pattern, picture, or room. It provides only the
types those things are values of. Each game supplies its own content, so two
games can share an engine without sharing a look. Every picture in this guide
is drawn by a pure function until the last step, which reads two from disk.

{2 One library}

{[
  open Camlcast
]}

This is the only library needed. {!Camlcast} contains everything a description
is made of. The platform underneath — [Engine], [Renderer], [Framebuffer],
[World], [Player] — is excluded. Reach it by adding [camlcast.core] to your
dune file; this makes bypassing the layer an explicit decision visible in a
diff, not something autocomplete offers. This guide never needs it.

{2 Three ways of being wrong}

A mistake in a level — a wall of no length, a doorway wider than the wall it is
cut into — raises [Invalid_argument] where you wrote it. These are bugs in your
program, not conditions it can be in.

An external failure — SDL refusing a window, a missing picture — comes back as
[Error (`Msg _)]. These are conditions.

The third kind is what {!Camlcast.Check} is for: a level that builds without
error and is still wrong. Examples: a room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a
wall, a step in the floor at a doorway. See Step 22.

{1 Step 0 — A project that depends on the engine}

The engine is not on opam yet, so pin it from git.

{@sh[
mkdir mygame && cd mygame
opam switch create . 5.5.0                # or use a switch you already have
eval $(opam env --switch=. --set-switch)  # so the pin lands in it, whatever
                                          # OPAMSWITCH was pointing at before
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git
]}

{b If the pin fails on macOS}, the cause is opam's depext check: opam cannot
detect Homebrew's SDL2 (HACKING.md in the repository explains why). Install
the system libraries yourself and skip the check:

{@sh[
brew install sdl2 sdl2_image
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git --no-depexts
]}

On Debian or Ubuntu, opam resolves [libsdl2-dev] and [libsdl2-image-dev]
through apt without help. On Windows, use the MSYS2 environment.

The project needs three files. [dune-project]:

{[
(lang dune 3.21)
(name mygame)
]}

[bin/dune]:

{[
(executable
 (name main)
 (libraries camlcast))
]}

And [bin/main.ml], which is Step 1.

{1 Step 1 — One room}

{[
  open Camlcast

  let checker ~color ~u ~v =
    Color.level color (if ((u / 16) + (v / 16)) land 1 = 0 then 240 else 170)

  let stone =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:(Texture.generate (checker ~color:(Color.rgb 150 150 160)))

  let ground =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:(Texture.generate (checker ~color:(Color.rgb 116 110 98)))

  let height = 4.
  let flat = Plane.horizontal 0.

  let level =
    P.(
      world ~atmosphere:Atmosphere.default
        [
          room ~height ~material:stone
            ~floor:(floor ~plane:flat ground)
            ~ceiling:(roof stone)
            ~outline:
              (corners
                 [
                   Vec.make (-6.) (-6.);
                   Vec.make 6. (-6.);
                   Vec.make 6. 6.;
                   Vec.make (-6.) 6.;
                 ])
            [ spawn (Vec.make (-4.5) 0.) ];
        ])

  let () =
    match Run.play ~title:"The Undercroft" level with
    | Ok _ending -> ()
    | Error (`Msg message) ->
        prerr_endline message;
        exit 1
]}

Run [dune exec bin/main.exe]. Walk with WASD and the mouse. Escape exits.

{2 What each piece is}

{!Camlcast.P} holds the parts a world is made of. Write it as a local open,
[P.( ... )], around a description rather than opening it over the whole file.
Its names are short and common — [wall], [room], [text] — so the local open
scopes them to the world description alone and marks where that description
starts and stops.

Distances are in {b cells}. One cell is one texture repeat. The eye stands
half a cell above the floor, so a twelve-cell vault under a four-cell ceiling
reads as a hall.

A pattern is a pure function from a texel coordinate to a colour.
{!Camlcast.Texture.generate} turns one into a tiling square.
{!Camlcast.Material.make} builds the material a wall or floor is rendered with.

A room is its [outline]: a {b closed} run of corners, and the walls between
them. {b Corner order does not matter}: the outline measures the loop and winds
it so the room is on the inside. A room wound the wrong way draws black from
within, and the symptom points nowhere near the cause — auto-winding is why you
will not meet it.

{!Camlcast.P.val-corners} marks every point as a plain corner, for an outline
that attaches nothing to any of its sides. The next step shows what a corner is
for.

The room's [height] and [material] are what a wall gets when its corner says
nothing, and the [ceiling] takes the height too: [roof stone] is a stone ceiling
at [height] above the floor, so the relation is stated once rather than at every
room. Say [~plane] or [~headroom] where it is not that.

{!Camlcast.P.val-spawn} is where the player starts, in the coordinates of the
room that holds it. There is no world-wide compass and no room to name: the room
it is written in is the room it means.

{!Camlcast.Run.play} opens a window, renders the description once per frame
until the player leaves, and reports the result. The [Error] case comes from
SDL — an external failure. The [Ok] case carries a {!Camlcast.Run.ending} this
game does not read yet; Step 24 does.

The whole program: [examples/step01_room.ml].

{b From step 23 you will be able to drag these.} The four corners above are
literals, and that is the whole of what makes them draggable: the editor draws
the room from over the top, and moving a corner rewrites the [Vec.make] that
put it there. Typing them is the way to learn what a corner is; the tool is for
the fiftieth one.

{1 Step 2 — Every wall its own}

A room applies its material to every side. Any corner may override it:

{[
  ~outline:[ corner sw ~material:brick; corner se; corner ne; corner nw ]
]}

{b A corner describes the wall {e leaving} it}: [corner sw ~material:brick] is
the wall from [sw] to [se] — the south wall, in brick. The other three fall
back to the room's. A corner accepts everything {!Camlcast.P.wall} takes — a
handler, decals, a height of its own. This lets a room's edge be decorated or
interactive without giving up auto-winding, which is the trade writing the walls
out one by one used to force.

Use {!Camlcast.P.wall} itself only for free-standing walls. This step adds a
plinth to the vault, low enough to see over, drawn from both sides:

{[
  wall ~height:1.1 ~material:slab (Vec.make (-2.) (-1.6)) (Vec.make (-2.) 1.6);
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step02_masonry.ml].
[demo/masonry.ml] shows one pattern function used at several colours.

{1 Step 3 — Pillars}

{!Camlcast.P.polygon} returns {e corners}, not walls: [sides] of them, [radius]
from [center] to each corner. So a pillar is an outline like the room's, and
{!Camlcast.P.block} is what stands one inside a room — a closed shape that is
not the room's edge and has no door cut into it. Winding is still automatic, and
any side can carry a handler.

{[
  let pillar center =
    P.block ~height ~material:slab
      (P.polygon ~center ~radius:0.7 ~sides:6 ~rotation:0.)
]}

Four of them ring the middle of the vault:

{[
  pillar (Vec.make 3. 3.);
  pillar (Vec.make 3. (-3.));
  pillar (Vec.make (-3.) 3.);
  pillar (Vec.make (-3.) (-3.));
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step03_pillars.ml]. [demo/haze.ml]
is a colonnade of them.

{1 Step 4 — The air}

A world has one {!Camlcast.Atmosphere}: how far you can see, what colour the
distance fades to, and where the light comes from. This step replaces
{!Camlcast.Atmosphere.default} with the undercroft's own dark atmosphere:

{[
  let air =
    Atmosphere.make ~haze:(Color.rgb 22 22 30) ~fog_distance:10.
      ~min_brightness:0.12 ~light:(Vec.make (-0.5) (-0.85)) ~ambient:0.3
      ~directional:0.7 ()
]}

[directional] sets how much of a wall's shading depends on which way it faces;
[ambient] sets how much does not. Under a high [directional] a six-sided pillar
reads as six different greys. Walk around one to see it.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step04_air.ml]. [demo/haze.ml] is
built to show exactly this.

{1 Step 5 — Pictures in the room}

A texture tiles a surface; an {!Camlcast.Image} is a picture with edges and an
alpha, drawn by the same kind of pure function:

{[
  let mural =
    Image.make ~width:48 (fun ~u ~v ->
        let ring r w =
          Image.disc ~cx:24. ~cy:24. ~r ~u ~v
          && not (Image.disc ~cx:24. ~cy:24. ~r:(r -. w) ~u ~v)
        in
        if ring 20. 3. || ring 11. 3. || Image.disc ~cx:24. ~cy:24. ~r:3. ~u ~v
        then (Color.rgb 208 178 122, 255)
        else Image.clear)
]}

A {b decal} is a picture fixed flat on a wall. It is placed in the wall's own
coordinates — [along] from the end the wall starts at, [z] up from the floor —
and attached to the corner that describes its wall:

{[
  corner sw ~material:brick
    ~decals:
      [ decal ~along:6. ~z:2. ~half_width:1.2 ~half_height:1.2 mural ];
]}

[~facing:Back] places the decal on the far face instead. [~glow] lights it
independently of the room's light.

A {b sprite} stands in the room and always faces the player. Its size sets its
height; its width comes from the picture. [~base] raises it above the floor.
[~glow] is how much light it emits itself, zero by default: a sprite without
glow is lit by the room's [ambient], so dimming a game's light dims its sprites
with it. This step adds a cold brazier on the floor and a mote floating near
the pillars:

{[
  sprite ~size:0.9 ~image:brazier_cold (Vec.make 0. 0.);
  sprite ~base:1.4 ~glow:0.9 ~size:0.25 ~image:mote (Vec.make 1.5 2.);
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step05_dressing.ml].
[demo/gallery.ml] and [demo/floating.ml] expand on this step.

{1 Step 6 — A second room}

Two rooms are joined by a {b door} in each and a {b connection} saying the two
are the same opening. A door is made once and cut into a leg of a room's
outline:

{[
  let east = P.door ~name:"east" ~width:2. ~clearance:2.6 ()
  let west = P.door ~name:"west" ~width:2. ~clearance:2.6 ()
]}

{b The outline stays closed and the door is cut out of it.} The leg is named by
its two corners, {e in either order} — the outline already says which side is
in, so the opening takes its winding from the wall rather than from the order
you wrote. The jambs left either side are legs of the outline like any other, so
a {!Camlcast.P.val-corner} dresses them.

{[
  ~outline:
    [
      corner sw ~material:brick ~decals:[ (* the mural *) ];
      corner se;
      corner ne;
      corner nw;
    ]

  (* among the room's children *)
  cut east ~along:(se, ne);
]}

The corridor is written in a frame of its own, because every room is:

{[
  let c_sw = Vec.make 0. (-2.)
  let c_se = Vec.make 8. (-2.)
  let c_ne = Vec.make 8. 2.
  let c_nw = Vec.make 0. 2.
]}

There is no world-wide coordinate frame; only the connection places the
corridor. Its floor is not written at all — a floor that gives no [plane] is
carried through the connection from the room on the other side, by the transform
that opening implies:

{[
  room ~height ~material:stone ~floor:(floor ground) ~ceiling:(roof stone)
    ~outline:(corners [ c_sw; c_se; c_ne; c_nw ])
    [ cut west ~along:(c_nw, c_sw) ];
]}

The engine carries it from the pair of ends it cut the opening at, so it cannot
be carried from the wrong pair — which was the mistake worth making impossible.
If the vault's floor later tilts, the corridor's follows. Give a room a [plane]
of its own to stop the carrying there.

{!Camlcast.Check} reports a step in the floor at a doorway either way.

Finally, the connection. It is a child of the world rather than of either room,
so joining two rooms and unjoining them touches neither:

{[
  connect east west;
]}

Nothing here names a room. A connection joins two doors, and a door is already
in one room, so there is nothing left for a name to do — {!Camlcast.P.val-room}
takes one only for diagnostics.

A door needs no connection to exist. One that has none leads nowhere {e yet}:
the renderer fills it with haze and it is solid to walk into, which is a level
part-built rather than a level wrong. Check says so as a warning.

{b Make the doors once, at the top level.} A door carries the identity its
connection joins by, and a description is rebuilt every frame — a door made
inside one is a different door each time. It is the rule
{!Camlcast_loom.Element.declare}'s page states, reached from another direction.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step06_antechamber.ml].
[demo/portals.ml] joins one room to a hub twice; [demo/slopes.ml] carries an
inclined floor through seamlessly.

{b A doorway that leads nowhere is a state, not a mistake.} The engine builds
one, the renderer fills it with haze, and {!Camlcast.Check} reports it as a
warning rather than an error — a level is built rooms first and ways between
them after, and refusing the half-built state would refuse the way anyone
works. The editor's graph panel draws the world as rooms and the ways between
them, marks the doorways that lead nowhere, and joins two of them in two
clicks. That is the part of a world hardest to hold in your head, and the only
part a picture can honestly show: there is no map, because
{!Camlcast.Vec}-space is a room's own and two rooms share none.

{1 Step 7 — Your first component}

Everything so far has been one value. A component is a function from props to
a description. Components are how a part of a world keeps state of its own.

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    P.sprite ~size:0.7 ~base:0.9 ~image:torch_stub pos
]}

Use it wherever a part of a world goes. Here, four times, at the four pillars:

{[
  torch ~key:"ne" (Vec.make 2.1 2.1);
  torch ~key:"se" (Vec.make 2.1 (-2.1));
  torch ~key:"nw" (Vec.make (-2.1) 2.1);
  torch ~key:"sw" (Vec.make (-2.1) (-2.1));
]}

{b Declare components at the top level.} This is a rule, not a style choice. A
component is identified by physical equality of its function, because the
function is all a component is. A component built inside another render is a
fresh closure every frame, so it is never the same component as last frame; it
is torn down and rebuilt along with everything under it, and state appears not
to work. {!Camlcast.Element.declare} exists partly to make the right form the
easy one. Its type also enforces the other rule that comes with identity: a
render function is monomorphic in its props. Props are one value; use a record
when there are several things to pass.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step07_component.ml].

{1 Step 8 — State}

{[
  let brazier =
    Element.declare ~name:"brazier" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state false in
    Events.use_pressed (Input.Key Key.space) (fun () -> set_lit true);
    P.sprite ~size:0.9
      ~glow:(if lit then 0.85 else 0.)
      ~image:(if lit then brazier_hot else brazier_cold)
      pos
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_state} returns the current value and a setter.
{!Camlcast.Events.use_pressed} runs a handler on the frame a control goes down
— the tap, not the hold. Space lights the brazier.

Two rules come with hooks.

{b Call hooks unconditionally, in the same order, every render.} Not inside an
[if], not inside a loop whose length varies. Nothing names a hook, so its
position is its identity. Breaking the rule raises
{!Camlcast.Hook.Hook_order_changed} where you broke it, rather than returning a
value that was never there.

{b A setter takes effect next frame.} It writes its slot and marks the tree; it
does not re-enter the render that is running. At sixty frames per second the
delay is sixteen milliseconds. In exchange, no render can fail to settle.

{b Walking uses no hook at all.} The bindings handle it, and it reaches the
description as movement without a component asking. Step 23 makes the bindings
themselves configurable.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step08_state.ml].

{b The editor can read this back.} {!Camlcast.Hook.use_state} takes a [~show],
which is a function turning the value into a line of text and is called by
nothing else:

{[
  let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state ~show:string_of_bool false
]}

A slot without one still says which hook made it and whether it changed this
frame — that much needs no type. What it holds is readable only where the
component says how, because a row of slots is untyped and a runtime that
guessed would have to call an [int], a [bool] and a [char] the same thing.

{1 Step 9 — Being looked at}

{!Camlcast.P.wall}, {!Camlcast.P.sprite} and {!Camlcast.P.cut} each take
[on_gaze] and [on_use], because those are the three things the crosshair can
land on — and a {!Camlcast.P.val-corner} takes them too, so a leg of an outline
is as interactive as a free-standing wall. The torches take both:

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state false in
    let eyed, set_eyed = Hook.use_state false in
    P.sprite ~size:0.7 ~base:0.9
      ~glow:(if lit then 0.9 else if eyed then 0.25 else 0.)
      ~image:(if lit then torch_flame else torch_stub)
      ~on_gaze:set_eyed
      ~on_use:(fun (spot : Aim.spot) ->
        if spot.distance <= reach then set_lit (not lit))
      pos
]}

[on_gaze] reports an {e enter} and a {e leave} — [true] when the crosshair
arrives, [false] when it goes, nothing in between. It is not a poll, so passing
a state setter directly works.

[on_use] fires when the player presses the use control — [E] by default —
while looking at this torch. The handler receives an {!Camlcast.Aim.spot}: the
distance, the number of doorways crossed, and for a wall the position on it.
The crosshair reaches as far as the rendered picture does — through doorways,
up to {!Camlcast.Config.max_portal_depth} of them. Whether a thing can be used
from here is therefore a rule the game writes itself; this one uses
[distance]:

{[
  let reach = 2.5
]}

{b Give a [key] to anything whose siblings can come and go.} The leave half of
[on_gaze] is the one thing in a frame that must recognise something from the
previous one, and an unkeyed child is recognised by its position among its
siblings. Insert a sibling ahead of it and the [false] lands on whatever now
occupies that position. A list written out in a fixed order is fine; a list
built from something that changes needs keys. The rule and the reason are in
{!Camlcast.P}, under "Being looked at".

This step adds a crosshair so there is something to aim with — one line of
HUD, which gets its own step:

{[
  hud [ crosshair () ];
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step09_gaze.ml].
[demo/targets.ml] refuses by [crossed] as well as [distance]; [demo/chalk.ml]
marks walls with the rest of the spot.

{b Keys are what the editor's tree panel is for.} A component mounted where it
should have been updated loses its state, the frame looks right, and nothing
says so. The panel counts it: a number climbing beside a component every frame
is one being rebuilt every frame, which almost always means it was declared
inside another component's render rather than at the top level.

{1 Step 10 — Time}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} runs once per frame and receives the previous
frame's duration. This step makes the brazier burn down over time. The
{e world's} atmosphere should follow the fire, so the fuel moves up into a
[game] component above the world. Put state at the lowest place everything
that reads it can reach.

{[
  let game =
    Element.declare ~name:"game" @@ fun () ->
    let fuel, set_fuel = Hook.use_state 0. in
    Events.use_pressed (Input.Key Key.space) (fun () -> set_fuel fuse);
    Events.use_frame (fun ~dt ->
        if fuel > 0. then set_fuel (Float.max 0. (fuel -. dt)));
    P.(
      world
        ~atmosphere:(air ~fire:(fuel /. fuse))
        [ (* the rooms, as before *) ])
]}

The atmosphere becomes a function of the fire. The brazier becomes a component
that is {e told} what it holds — props down, state up:

{[
  type brazier_props = { fuel : float; pos : Vec.t }

  let brazier =
    Element.declare ~name:"brazier" @@ fun { fuel; pos } ->
    P.sprite ~size:0.9
      ~glow:(if fuel > 0. then 0.85 else 0.)
      ~image:(if fuel > 0. then brazier_hot else brazier_cold)
      pos
]}

and [Run.play] is handed [(game ())] instead of a value.

Scale per-frame work by [dt]. A frame's length is not a constant, and a game
that ignores it runs at the speed of the machine. The handler is also called
once with [dt = 0.] before the first frame is drawn, because the description
must be rendered once to know where to spawn the player. Work scaled by [dt]
costs nothing in that call.

Effects — {!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} is one — run {e after} the scene is
assembled and never during a render, because a render must be a pure function
of props and state. A handler that set state mid-render would make what a
frame shows depend on the order its components happened to be walked in.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step10_burning.ml].
[demo/phases.ml] uses the same structure with different content.

{1 Step 11 — A box, for what merely accumulates}

This step turns the floating mote into a wisp that slowly circles the brazier:

{[
  let wisp =
    Element.declare ~name:"wisp" @@ fun (center : Vec.t) ->
    let phase = Hook.use_ref 0. in
    Events.use_frame (fun ~dt -> phase := !phase +. dt);
    let pos = Vec.add center (Vec.scale (Vec.of_angle (0.45 *. !phase)) 2.6) in
    P.sprite ~key:"wisp"
      ~base:(1.3 +. (0.25 *. sin !phase))
      ~glow:0.9 ~size:0.25 ~image:mote pos
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_ref} is a box made once and returned unchanged every
render. Writing it requests no frame; that is the whole difference from state.
A ref fits here {e because the loop renders every frame anyway}: a phase that
grows continuously would make every [set_state] a request for a frame already
coming. State is for what arrives in steps and must be shown; a ref is for
what merely accumulates while the world is redrawn regardless. A root that
renders on demand — {!Camlcast.Mount}, step 22 — sees the difference: writing
a ref never wakes it.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step11_wisp.ml]. [demo/dust.ml]
moves a few hundred of these a frame.

{1 Step 12 — Drawing over the world}

{[
  let w, h = Events.use_viewport () in
  let dt = Events.use_dt () in
  let shown = Hook.use_ref 0. in
  shown := !shown +. (((fuel /. fuse) -. !shown) *. Float.min 1. (6. *. dt));
]}

and, at the end of the world's children:

{[
  hud
    [
      rect ~alpha:140
        ~x:(((w - 120) / 2) - 4)
        ~y:(h - 24) ~w:128 ~h:16 ~color:(Color.rgb 10 10 14) ();
      bar
        ~x:((w - 120) / 2)
        ~y:(h - 20) ~w:120 ~h:8 ~fraction:!shown
        ~color:(Color.rgb 230 170 80) ();
      highlight ();
      crosshair ();
    ];
]}

{!Camlcast.P.hud} is a child of [world], drawn over the finished frame in the
order it is written. {!Camlcast.P.rect} draws a rectangle and {!Camlcast.P.bar}
draws a bar. {!Camlcast.P.highlight} outlines whatever the crosshair is on: a
rectangle around a sprite, and for a picture on a wall the trapezoid its
corners project to, because a wall recedes in perspective.

Coordinates are the {b buffer's}, not the window's. The engine renders at
whatever whole-number fraction of the window keeps it under
{!Camlcast.Config.max_render_height} and stretches the result, so a
thousand-pixel window is commonly a five-hundred-pixel buffer.
{!Camlcast.Events.use_viewport} returns the frame's real size. The meter's
centring is computed from it.

{!Camlcast.Events.use_dt} returns the same [dt] the frame handler gets, read
during the render. Here it eases the drawn fraction toward the true one at a
rate bounded by the actual frame rate.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step12_hud.ml].
[demo/overlay.ml] shows everything the layer can draw on one page.

{1 Step 13 — Words}

The engine ships no font, just as it ships no colours or pictures. The game
supplies one: an atlas picture and the grid it is laid out on.

{[
  let typeface =
    Result.map
      (fun atlas ->
        Font.make ~fallback:'\127' ~atlas ~width:6 ~height:10 ~first:32 ())
      (Image.of_asset "assets/font.png")

  let () =
    match typeface with
    | Error (`Msg reason) ->
        prerr_endline reason;
        exit 1
    | Ok font -> (
        match Run.play ~title:"The Undercroft" (game font) with
        (* ... *))
]}

This is the guide's first picture from disk. {!Camlcast.Image.of_asset} looks
for [assets/font.png] relative to the executable. Run [dune build] once first
— it copies [assets/] beside the examples in [_build] — or point
[CAMLCAST_ASSETS] at a directory that holds it. A missing file is the second
way of being wrong: it comes back as [Error] and is handled before any window
opens. The font travels into [game] as props.

While the brazier is cold, the game shows an explanatory message, wrapped to
the buffer width:

{[
  let lines =
    Hook.use_memo ~deps:w (fun () -> Font.wrap font message ~width:(w - 24))
  in
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_memo} returns [compute ()], recomputed only when [deps]
changes. Wrapping walks every character, so compute it once per width rather
than once per frame. The lines are drawn with {!Camlcast.P.text} when [fuel]
is spent, and not drawn otherwise; the hook itself runs every render,
unconditionally, per the hook rule. {!Camlcast.Font.measure} is the other half
of the text arithmetic; it centres the prompt in the next step.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step13_words.ml].
[demo/text.ml] wraps, measures and clips a whole page; [demo/typeface.ml]
defines the same atlas once, shared by the other demos.

{1 Step 14 — Asking what the eye is on}

[on_gaze] {e tells} the thing being looked at. {!Camlcast.Events.use_aim} lets
the description {e ask}: what was the crosshair on when the last frame was
drawn, and where on it. Use it to react to whatever is looked at without that
thing's involvement:

{[
  let prompt =
    match Events.use_aim () with
    | Some { Aim.where = Aim.On_sprite; distance; _ } when distance <= reach ->
        Some "press E"
    | _ -> None
  in
]}

An {!Camlcast.Aim.spot} carries the kind and the geometry — [On_wall] with its
[along] and [z], [On_sprite], [On_doorway] — and never the identity. The
prompt therefore appears for any sprite within reach, and each torch's own
[on_use] still decides what E does there. The answer is one frame late, like
the viewport, for the same reason: a description is rendered before the player
moves through the world it describes.

The prompt is centred with {!Camlcast.Font.measure}:

{[
  (match prompt with
  | None -> Element.empty
  | Some line ->
      let tw, _ = Font.measure font line in
      text ~font ~x:((w - tw) / 2) ~y:(h - 36) line);
]}

[Element.empty] describes {e nothing}. A component cannot decline to return,
so absence is a value too.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step14_prompt.ml].

{1 Step 15 — A door in the doorway}

This step adds a courtyard past the corridor, closed off by an iron grille.
The grille is a {!Camlcast.Texture.generate_masked} material — colour where
the bars are, alpha [0] between them — so the way out is visible before it
opens:

{[
  let grille =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:
        (Texture.generate_masked (fun ~u ~v ->
             if u mod 16 < 3 || v mod 32 < 3 then (Color.rgb 44 46 52, 255)
             else (Color.rgb 0 0 0, 0)))
]}

A {!Camlcast.Door} is a material and a state, [Open] or [Closed], and it hangs
in an opening. Here the corridor's east end wants a {e brick} lintel over
{e stone} jambs — the strip over an opening is otherwise made of the wall it
was cut into, and this one should read as a transom rather than as more wall.

Both are arguments to the cut. The corridor is open at both ends now, and that
is still one closed outline with two doors cut into it:

{[
  let west = P.door ~name:"west" ~width:2. ~clearance:2.6 ()
  let gate_out = P.door ~name:"gate" ~width:2. ~clearance:2.6 ()
]}

{[
  ~outline:(corners [ c_sw; c_se; c_ne; c_nw ])

  (* among the room's children *)
  cut west ~along:(c_sw, c_nw);
  cut gate_out
    ~leaf:(Door.make ~state:Closed grille)
    ~lintel:{ top = height; material = brick }
    ~along:(c_ne, c_se);
]}

[clearance] is the opening's height and it lives on the door, because the two
sides of one opening have to agree about it. The {!Camlcast.lintel} and the
leaf live on the cut, because they are how {e this} room presents the opening
and two rooms are allowed to present it differently — the courtyard hangs the
same leaf and says nothing about a lintel, so its side takes its own wall's.

{b What must agree is on the door; what may differ is on the cut.}

The courtyard cuts its side of the same opening and hangs the same leaf, and a
connection says the two are one:

{[
  cut gate_in ~leaf:(Door.make ~state:Closed grille) ~along:(y_sw, y_nw);
  (* ... *)
  connect gate_out gate_in;
]}

A leaf open from one side and shut from the other is a door you could walk
through in only one direction, so {!Camlcast.Check} objects if the two
disagree. Its floor needs no carrying: a room that gives no [plane] takes its
neighbour's.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step15_gate.ml]. [demo/doors.ml]
opens and shuts doors from both sides; [demo/barred.ml] is a room of grilles;
[demo/glass.ml] demonstrates the translucent pass.

{1 Step 16 — Held controls}

This step adds a winch that opens the gate. It uses the two ways of reading
input other than the tap.

{[
  let cupped = Events.use_down (Input.Key Key.lshift) in
  let actions = Events.use_actions () in
  let winding = at_winch && Input.down actions (Input.Key Key.e) in
  let wound = Input.held_for actions (Input.Key Key.e) in
]}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_down} is the hold: whether a control is down right now,
read during the render. Holding Shift {e cups} the brazier's flame: the frame
handler stops draining fuel while it is held.

{!Camlcast.Events.use_actions} returns the whole {!Camlcast.Input.actions}
record, for questions the two hooks do not cover. {!Camlcast.Input.held_for}
reports how long E has been held. The frame handler opens the gate once E has
been held long enough at the winch:

{[
  Events.use_frame (fun ~dt ->
      if fuel > 0. && not cupped then set_fuel (Float.max 0. (fuel -. dt));
      if winding && wound >= winch_takes && not gate_open then
        set_gate_open true);
]}

The winch itself is a sprite whose gaze handler sets the {e game's} state,
because what it opens is the game's gate. Both doors' state now follows
[gate_open]:

{[
  sprite ~key:"winch" ~size:0.8 ~base:0.8 ~image:wheel ~on_gaze:set_at_winch
    (Vec.make 7.2 1.3);
]}

Both hooks are named after {!Camlcast.Input.pressed} and
{!Camlcast.Input.val-down}; they ask those two questions of the current frame.
The API takes a control rather than a key throughout, so a mouse button can do
the same job as a key. Step 23 does exactly that.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step16_winch.ml].
[demo/controls.ml] prints what it is asked with [Key.name].

{1 Step 17 — Crossing a doorway}

{[
  Events.use_crossed (fun crossing ->
      set_last_crossing (Some crossing);
      if crossing.Events.to_room = "corridor" then set_visited true;
      (* ... *));
]}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_crossed} runs once for each doorway the last frame went
through, at the same point in a frame that {!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} runs.
A crossing identifies its rooms and doorways by the names the description gave
them, not by index: a world is rebuilt every frame, and an index is an
artifact of one assembly. Crossings are one frame late, like everything read
back from the world, and {b empty while a description is placing the camera}
— placing the eye is a jump, not a walk, so no doorway is crossed along any
path. {!Camlcast.Events.use_crossings} is the same list, read rather than
handled.

The corridor records its first visit: motes hang in it from the first crossing
on. The status line in the corner reads three parts of the frame at once,
which is what {!Camlcast.Events.use} is for — the whole
{!Camlcast.Events.type-t} record in one call:

{[
  let frame = Events.use () in
  let status =
    let ms = int_of_float (frame.Events.dt *. 1000.) in
    let bw, bh = frame.Events.viewport in
    (* ... *)
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step17_crossings.ml].
[demo/trail.ml] and [demo/endless.ml] build further on crossings.

{1 Step 18 — Reaching outside}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_effect} is where a component may reach outside the
description — a log, a subscription, a file. It runs after the frame is
committed, never during a render. [deps] decides when it runs again. The
returned cleanup lives exactly as long as what it cleans:

{[
  Hook.use_effect ~deps:gate_open (fun () ->
      if gate_open then prerr_endline "the gate grinds open";
      None);
]}

runs after the first frame and again each time [gate_open] changes. The wisp
takes the other form: [~deps:()] means once, on mount, with the cleanup run
when the component leaves the tree:

{[
  Hook.use_effect ~deps:() (fun () ->
      prerr_endline "a wisp drifts in";
      Some (fun () -> prerr_endline "the wisp departs"));
]}

The wisp leaves the tree when the gate opens:

{[
  (if gate_open then Element.empty else wisp (Vec.make 0. 0.));
]}

Raise the gate and the departure line prints on stderr. A subscription taken
in the effect and dropped in the cleanup would have lived exactly that long;
the next step relies on this. To run an effect every frame instead, pass
[~equal:(fun _ _ -> false)]. The default is structural equality, which raises
on deps that hold a closure, so those need an [equal] of their own.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step18_effect.ml].

{1 Step 19 — Shared state, by hand}

Component state belongs to a component. This step scatters embers through the
undercroft and counts them, and the count belongs to no single component.
Threading it down as props would add arguments that exist only to be passed
on. The count therefore lives outside the tree, in fifteen deliberately bare
lines:

{[
  let embers_taken = ref 0
  let listeners : (unit -> unit) list ref = ref []

  let subscribe listener =
    listeners := listener :: !listeners;
    fun () -> listeners := List.filter (fun l -> l != listener) !listeners

  let take_ember () =
    incr embers_taken;
    List.iter (fun listener -> listener ()) !listeners
]}

An ember keeps its own taken state and notifies listeners of the count:

{[
  let ember =
    Element.declare ~name:"ember" @@ fun (pos : Vec.t) ->
    let taken, set_taken = Hook.use_state false in
    if taken then Element.empty
    else
      P.sprite ~size:0.2 ~base:0.05 ~glow:0.8 ~image:ember_img
        ~on_use:(fun (spot : Aim.spot) ->
          if spot.distance <= reach then begin
            set_taken true;
            take_ember ()
          end)
        pos
]}

The tally uses the last hook. {!Camlcast.Hook.use_invalidate} returns a
function that tells this component's root it has work to do — the integration
point for anything the runtime does not own. Subscribe in an effect,
invalidate from the callback, unsubscribe in the cleanup:

{[
  let tally =
    Element.declare ~name:"tally" @@ fun (font : Font.t) ->
    let invalidate = Hook.use_invalidate () in
    Hook.use_effect ~deps:() (fun () -> Some (subscribe invalidate));
    let w, _ = Events.use_viewport () in
    let line = Printf.sprintf "embers %d" !embers_taken in
    let tw, _ = Font.measure font line in
    P.text ~font ~color:(Color.rgb 236 150 90) ~x:(w - tw - 12) ~y:12 line
]}

Under {!Camlcast.Run.play} every frame renders anyway, so the tally would
appear to work without any of this. The invalidate makes it {e correct} rather
than coincidental: a root that renders on demand — {!Camlcast.Mount}, three
steps from here — redraws this text only because something asked.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step19_embers.ml]. The manual
plumbing is the point of the step; read the next step before adopting it.

{1 Step 20 — The store}

{!Camlcast.Store} packages step 19's pattern: the state, the one function
allowed to change it, and the listeners.

{[
  type progress = { embers : int }
  type action = Ember_taken

  let advance state Ember_taken = { embers = state.embers + 1 }
  let game_store = Store.create ~initial:{ embers = 0 } ~reducer:advance
]}

Create the store once, at the top level. A store made inside a render would be
a new store every frame, holding [initial] forever. The ember replaces its
hand-rolled notify with {!Camlcast.Store.dispatch}, safe to call from any
handler or effect and never from inside a render:

{[
  Store.dispatch game_store Ember_taken
]}

and the tally replaces subscribe-invalidate-cleanup with one hook:

{[
  let embers = Store.use_selector game_store (fun s -> s.embers) in
]}

{!Camlcast.Store.use_selector} wakes the component exactly when the selected
value changes. Select the smallest value that suffices: a component reading
[fun s -> s.embers] is untouched by an action that changes anything else,
while one reading [fun s -> s] wakes on every action. There is no provider and
no dispatch hook, because OCaml needs neither: the store is a module-level
value, reachable by name.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step20_store.ml].

{1 Step 21 — Context}

Some values are {e surroundings} rather than state: a palette, a typeface, how
alive the flames are. Threading them as props has the same cost the store
avoided, but these values vary with position in the tree, not with time.
{!Camlcast.Context} handles that case.

{[
  let flicker = Context.make 1.
]}

The argument is the default value, used by a render with no provider above it;
{!Camlcast.Check} renders with the default. The game binds the context above
the world, so cupping the brazier now dims every flame at once:

{[
  let alive = if cupped then 0.35 else 1. in
  (* ... *)
  Element.provide flicker alive
  @@ [ P.(world (* ... *)) ]
]}

Any component under it reads the value back, with nothing in between naming
it:

{[
  let alive = Hook.use_context flicker in
]}

{!Camlcast.Hook.use_context} claims no slot and answers the same way every
render, so it is the one hook that is harmless inside a conditional. Write it
unconditionally anyway; one rule is easier to keep than a rule with
exceptions. Nothing subscribes and nothing needs to: a description is rebuilt
every frame, so a changed value is simply what the next render sees.
{!Camlcast.Events.context} — how time and input reach the hooks — is itself a
context, bound by {!Camlcast.Run.play} around your description every frame.

This step also introduces {!Camlcast.Element.fragment}, for a helper that
returns several primitives where one is expected. The corridor's pair of motes
is now one element to the description. The key on the fragment keeps the pair
as a whole in its place when the list around it changes:

{[
  let mote_pair ~key a b =
    Element.fragment ~key
      [
        P.sprite ~key:"a" ~base:1.2 ~glow:0.7 ~size:0.2 ~image:mote a;
        P.sprite ~key:"b" ~base:1.6 ~glow:0.7 ~size:0.2 ~image:mote b;
      ]
]}

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step21_context.ml].

{1 Step 22 — Prove it without a window}

Everything in a description can be inspected without SDL. The step-15 door
disagreement was found exactly this way. The game now checks itself before it
plays:

{[
  let prove font =
    let level = game font in
    (match Check.report level with
    | [] -> ()
    | found ->
        prerr_endline (Check.format found);
        exit 1);
    let built = Mount.build level in
    assert (not built.Scene.finished);
    let mount = Mount.create () in
    let frame dt =
      Element.provide Events.context { Events.still with dt } [ game font ]
    in
    ignore (Mount.render mount (frame 0.));
    ignore (Mount.render mount (frame 0.1));
    Mount.destroy mount;
    let scratch = Store.create ~initial:{ embers = 0 } ~reducer:advance in
    Store.dispatch scratch Ember_taken;
    assert ((Store.state scratch).embers = 1);
    print_endline "the undercroft holds: ok"
]}

{!Camlcast.Check.report} renders the description — components and effects
included, with effect cleanups run afterwards — and reads the assembled world
for what a compiler cannot see: a room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a
wall, two doorways joined to the same third, a door only one side declares.
Where it can, a diagnostic names the {e component} that wrote the offending
part rather than the room the engine found it in.

{!Camlcast.Mount} is the rest of the loop with the window left out.
{!Camlcast.Mount.build} gives one look at the {!Camlcast.Scene} a description
assembles to. {!Camlcast.Mount.create} and {!Camlcast.Mount.render} keep state
between renders. A fake frame is nothing but {!Camlcast.Events.context} bound
by hand — [Events.still] with a [dt] — and that is how time passes in a test.
The scratch store shows the last piece: a reducer is a plain function, so the
shared state is as provable as the geometry.

None of this needs a window, so in your own project it belongs in
[dune runtest] — one case per level covers your levels. Here it runs at
startup, and

{@sh[
dune exec examples/step22_check.exe -- --check
]}

runs only the proof.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step22_check.ml].

{b The same diagnostics appear on the plan.} {!Camlcast.Check.t} carries a
[spot] — a room and a point — and anything with one is marked where it is
rather than only described. A step in the floor at a doorway is much easier to
believe when it is drawn on the doorway.

{1 Step 23 — Controls, and the map}

Everything the loop acts on by itself — walking, looking, leaving, the use
control and the map — is one record, {!Camlcast.Controls.t}, handed to
{!Camlcast.Run.play}:

{[
  let controls =
    let hold key weight =
      { Binding.source = Binding.Hold (Input.Key key); weight }
    in
    Controls.make
      ~bindings:
        (Binding.make
           ~forward:
             {
               Binding.speed = 3.6;
               terms =
                 [
                   hold Key.w 1.; hold Key.s (-1.); hold Key.i 1.;
                   hold Key.k (-1.);
                 ];
             }
           ~leave:[ Input.Key Key.escape ] ())
      ~use:[ Input.Key Key.e; Input.Button Input.Left ]
      ~map:[ Input.Key Key.f3; Input.Key Key.m ]
      ()
]}

A {!Camlcast.Binding} axis is a list of weighted sources summed into one
number that scales the per-second speed. Here walking gains a second pair of
keys, and the left mouse button starts working what E works. {b Replacement,
not merging}, at every level: a given [~forward] is the whole of that axis, so
W and S are restated beside I and K. A given [~bindings] is the whole of the
bindings, so Escape is restated too; {!Camlcast.Controls.default} previously
supplied it.

Press {b F3} — or now {b M} — in the running game and the room is drawn from
above: every wall with a short tick pointing the way its normal faces,
doorways green when linked and red when they lead nowhere, the player's disc
with its collision ring, and a mark on anything {!Camlcast.Check} flags. The
ticks are the fastest way to see an outline wound wrong — a row of them
pointing out of the room. Auto-winding is why the map has not been needed so
far. [~map:[]] disables the map, and it costs that game nothing: the walk over
every wall that feeds the map only happens while the map is up.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step23_controls.ml].
[demo/bindings.ml] and [demo/controls.ml] go further.

{2 The studio}

The map shows the room from above so that a wall pointing the wrong way is
visible. The studio is what happens when that picture is allowed to answer back.

Two lines of a game, and one line of its [dune]:

{[
  (preprocess (pps ppx_camlcast))
]}

{[
  let studio = Camlcast_edit.Session.create ()

  (* in the world *)      Camlcast_edit.Session.pointer studio
  (* in the hud *)        Camlcast_edit.Session.overlay studio ()
  (* instead of Run.play *) Camlcast_edit.Session.play studio level
]}

The preprocessor is what makes the difference between looking and touching. It
stamps each constructor with the place in your source it was written at, and
without it the panels still draw and nothing can be changed.

No font is passed, and none is needed: the panel falls back to the one
[camlcast.edit] carries. A developer's overlay has to be readable before the
game it is over has any art, so this is the one place the engine's rule about
holding no content stops — and a shipped game does not link this library at
all. Pass [~font] where the game has a face it would rather see.

Three panels, and the overlay reads its own keys — [F5] the plan, [F6] the
graph, [F7] the tree, [F8] closes it, [Tab] moves the plan to the next room.

[F5] rather than [F1] because reading a key here does not take it away from the
run: the map above is still on [F3], and a panel there would draw itself and the
map of the same room on the same frame. [F1] through [F4] are left for you.

The {b plan} is this room from above, and dragging a corner rewrites the
[Vec.make] that put it there. With something picked, [\[] and [\]] walk its
fields and [-] and [=] move the chosen one, which is how a height or a glow is
changed — those are numbers, but they are not places, and there is nowhere on a
plan to drag them to. [u] takes the last change back.

The {b graph} is the rooms and the ways between them, where two clicks join a
doorway that leads nowhere to another. The {b tree} is what your description
came to and what each component is holding, and it is where a component being
rebuilt every frame shows up as a number that climbs.

[x] moves whatever is picked into a component of its own, in a file named after
its [~key]. Named after the key because that is already there and is already
what you called the thing: the engine reports keys as places on a keyboard and
has no text input, so a name asked for would have to be spelled out of
scancodes.

{2 What can be changed, and what only drawn}

One rule decides it: {b a thing is editable if it was written as a literal}.

{[
  wall ~height:2.5 ~material:stone (Vec.make (-3.) 1.) (Vec.make 3. 1.)
]}

Every argument there is written down, so every one of them can be changed — the
ends by dragging, the height with a key. Against that:

{[
  sprite ~size:0.9 ~glow:(if lit then 0.8 else 0.) ~image:flame (Vec.make 2. 3.)
]}

The glow is worked out from state. There is no line to write a new one into,
because what is written there is a rule and not a value. So the sprite is drawn
and can be dragged by its position, and its glow is shown and greyed. Per
argument, not per thing.

The same answer covers a room's outline. Its legs are walls the engine built
from your corners — {!Camlcast.P.room} makes them, not you — so they are drawn
and are not draggable, and what an editor offers instead is the outline they
came from. Nothing here refuses a file for being written the way you wanted;
what it cannot change, it says it cannot change.

{2 What it writes}

Your file, with one expression different. The editor parses to find out {e
where} things are and then replaces those bytes: comments, blank lines and
whatever [ocamlformat] had already made of the file all survive, because
nothing reconstructs them. A change that would leave text which does not parse
is refused and the file is not written.

Which is why the sentence at the top of this guide is still true. There is no
level file format. What the editor saves is the OCaml you would have typed.

{1 Step 24 — The ending}

This step opens the courtyard to the sky. A ceiling is either a roof or the
absence of one, and absence names what shows instead:

{[
  let evening = Sky.make ~sun_azimuth:2.7 ~sun_height:0.06 ()
]}

{[
  ~ceiling:(open_sky evening)
]}

Stepping out is a crossing, and it starts a timer:

{[
  if crossing.Events.to_room = "courtyard" && freedom = None then
    set_freedom (Some 0.)
]}

While the timer runs, the description places the eye itself:

{[
  (match freedom with
  | None -> Element.empty
  | Some t ->
      camera ~room:"courtyard" ~pos:(Vec.make 5. 0.)
        ~angle:(2.6 +. (0.2 *. t))
        ~pitch:(Float.min 0.35 (0.12 *. t))
        ());
]}

While a {!Camlcast.P.camera} is in a description, the controls do not move the
player at all, because the description sets the eye position every frame and
unrequested movement would conflict with it. Nor is the world interacted with
through this eye: [on_gaze], [on_use] and the crossings all stay silent, so a
pan across a room does not fire an enter and a leave on everything it sweeps
past. See {!Camlcast.Run.aiming} for that rule stated once. Remove the camera
and the player carries on from wherever it was last put.

After three seconds, the description declares the ending:

{[
  (match freedom with Some t when t >= 3. -> finish | _ -> Element.empty);
]}

{!Camlcast.P.finish} is declared, not called: a component that has reached its
ending describes an ending, in the same place and the same way it describes
everything else. The frame it appears in is drawn, and then the run stops. The
[ending] that [Run.play] has returned all along is now read:

{[
  match Run.play ~title:"The Undercroft" ~controls (game font) with
  | Ok Returned ->
      Printf.printf "out of the dark, carrying %d embers\n"
        (Store.state game_store).embers
  | Ok Closed -> ()
  | Error (`Msg reason) ->
      prerr_endline reason;
      exit 1
]}

[Returned] means the run ended on its own terms — [finish], or the leave
control. [Closed] means the window was closed. The next step depends on this
difference.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step24_escape.ml].
[demo/daylight.ml] is two rooms under different skies.

{1 Step 25 — A launcher}

A window and a run have separate lifetimes. {!Camlcast.Run.play} combines
{!Camlcast.Run.with_window} and {!Camlcast.Run.on}. A title screen comes from
using them separately:

{[
  let () =
    (* ... *)
    Run.with_window ~title:"The Undercroft" (fun window ->
        let rec descend () =
          match Run.on window ~controls:title_controls (title_screen font) with
          | Ok Returned -> (
              match Run.on window ~controls (game font) with
              | Ok Returned ->
                  Printf.printf "out of the dark, carrying %d embers\n"
                    (Store.state game_store).embers;
                  descend ()
              | other -> other)
          | other -> other
        in
        descend ())
]}

Title and game take turns on one window. Finishing the game returns to the
title, and the picture changes without the window closing and reopening. The
store outlives every run, so the embers accumulate across descents.

The title screen is a world like any other — a brick foyer, a torch, a camera
swaying where a player would stand — plus the one part of {!Camlcast.P} this
game has not needed. {!Camlcast.P.cursor} frees the mouse for a screen drawn
over the world, instead of capturing it for looking around. While the cursor
is up, nothing in the world can be interacted with — the crosshair is not the
player's. {!Camlcast.Events.use_aim} still answers, because it asks rather
than being told. Enter describes [finish], and on a shared window [finish]
hands the window back:

{[
  Events.use_pressed (Input.Key Key.return) (fun () -> set_descending true);
  (* ... *)
  cursor;
  (if descending then finish else Element.empty);
]}

The title's controls list no leave binding, so Enter and the close button are
the only two ways out of it. Controls are per-run; that is the other
capability this step gains.

The whole program as of this step: [examples/step25_launcher.ml].
[demo/menu.ml] and [bin/demo.ml] use the same structure at full size.

{1 Step 26 — Shipping}

So far the game has drawn everything except the font. Real art loads the same
way the font did. Everything read from disk gathers into one record and one
failure path:

{[
  type art = { font : Font.t; tiles : Material.t; poster : Image.t }

  let load_art () =
    let ( let* ) = Result.bind in
    let* atlas = Image.of_asset "assets/font.png" in
    let* tiles = Texture.of_asset "assets/tiles.png" in
    let* poster = Image.of_asset "assets/poster.png" in
    Ok
      {
        font = Font.make ~fallback:'\127' ~atlas ~width:6 ~height:10 ~first:32 ();
        tiles = Material.make ~pattern:tiles;
        poster;
      }
]}

The south wall uses the tiles, the mural becomes the poster, and a missing
file reports its name before any window opens. A bundled game needs exactly
this behaviour.

{!Camlcast.Asset} defines the search order: [Contents/Resources] for a bundle,
beside the binary, one directory above it, and [share/<exe>] — plus wherever
[CAMLCAST_ASSETS] points, which takes precedence over all of these.
[dune build] therefore serves a checkout. Shipping is
[dune build --profile release]
plus putting the binary, the SDL2 libraries and [assets/] in one directory;
[tools/bundle-*.sh] in this repository does exactly that, per platform.

The whole program, and the whole game: [examples/step26_shipping.ml].
[demo/loading.ml] loads pictures from disk with nothing else involved.

